### What is the Wildcard Matching Problem?

Implement pattern matching where `?` matches any single character and `*` matches any sequence of characters including the empty sequence. The match must cover the entire input string.

At National Bank of India, a batch processor filters statement files by a glob pattern like `stmt_2024_*.csv` or `report_??_final.pdf`.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class WildcardMatching {
    public static boolean isMatch(String s, String p) {
        int m = s.length(), n = p.length();
        boolean[][] dp = new boolean[m + 1][n + 1];
        dp[0][0] = true;

        // First row: leading stars match empty text
        for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
            if (p.charAt(j - 1) == '*') {
                dp[0][j] = dp[0][j - 1];
            }
        }

        for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
                char pc = p.charAt(j - 1);
                if (pc == '*') {
                    dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j]     // star absorbs one more char
                            || dp[i][j - 1];     // star matches empty
                } else if (pc == '?' || pc == s.charAt(i - 1)) {
                    dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1]; // single char match
                }
            }
        }
        return dp[m][n];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(isMatch("statement_2024", "stat*2024"));   // true
        System.out.println(isMatch("report_ab", "report_??"));       // true
        System.out.println(isMatch("data", "da*ta*"));               // true
    }
}
```

```text
true
true
true
```

### Why Is This Simpler Than Regex Matching?

Wildcard Matching is simpler because the star is standalone: it matches any sequence by itself, not tied to a preceding element. In Regex Matching, `a*` means zero or more `a`s, so the star modifies the previous character and the DP must look back two positions. Here, the star directly absorbs characters or matches empty.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class WildcardVsRegex {
    // Wildcard *: dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j] || dp[i][j-1]
    //   - absorb one char OR match empty
    //   - standalone, no preceding element needed

    // Regex *: dp[i][j] = dp[i][j-2] || (prevMatch && dp[i-1][j])
    //   - zero occurrences of preceding element OR one more occurrence
    //   - tied to preceding element
}
```

### How Does the Star Case Work?

The star has two branches. `dp[i-1][j]` means the star absorbs the current text character and keeps matching (star stays, text advances). `dp[i][j-1]` means the star matches the empty sequence (pattern advances past star, text stays).

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class StarCaseWildcard {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Text: "abc", Pattern: "a*c"
        // Star at j=2:
        //   dp[1][2] = dp[0][2] (absorb 'a') || dp[1][1] (match empty)
        //   dp[2][2] = dp[1][2] (absorb 'b') || dp[2][1] (match empty)
        //   dp[3][2] = dp[2][2] (absorb 'c') || dp[3][1] (match empty)
        System.out.println(WildcardMatching.isMatch("abc", "a*c"));  // true
    }
}
```

```text
true
```

### How Is This Different from Regex Matching?

| Aspect | Wildcard Matching | Regex Matching |
|---|---|---|
| Star meaning | Any sequence (standalone) | Zero or more of preceding element |
| Star zero case | dp[i][j-1] (skip star) | dp[i][j-2] (skip x* pair) |
| Star one-more case | dp[i-1][j] (absorb char) | dp[i-1][j] if preceding matches |
| Single match | `?` or exact char | `.` or exact char |
| Complexity | O(m*n) | O(m*n) |
| Space-optimal variant | Greedy two-pointer O(1) space | No known O(1) space variant |

### What Are the Edge Cases?

Edge cases include: pattern is a single star (matches everything), pattern has no wildcards (exact match required), text is empty with a star pattern (matches), and consecutive stars.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class WildcardEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(WildcardMatching.isMatch("anything", "*"));     // true
        System.out.println(WildcardMatching.isMatch("exact", "exact"));    // true
        System.out.println(WildcardMatching.isMatch("exact", "exac"));     // false
        System.out.println(WildcardMatching.isMatch("", "***"));           // true
        System.out.println(WildcardMatching.isMatch("", "?"));             // false
    }
}
```

```text
true
true
false
true
false
```

### What About the Greedy Two-Pointer Approach?

The greedy approach uses two pointers (one for text, one for pattern) and remembers the position of the last star and the text position when it was encountered. When a mismatch occurs, it backtracks to the last star and advances the text pointer by one.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class WildcardGreedy {
    public static boolean isMatch(String s, String p) {
        int si = 0, pi = 0;
        int starIdx = -1, matchIdx = -1;

        while (si < s.length()) {
            if (pi < p.length() && (p.charAt(pi) == '?' || p.charAt(pi) == s.charAt(si))) {
                si++; pi++;  // single char match
            } else if (pi < p.length() && p.charAt(pi) == '*') {
                starIdx = pi; matchIdx = si;  // remember star position
                pi++;  // try matching empty first
            } else if (starIdx != -1) {
                pi = starIdx + 1;  // backtrack to after star
                matchIdx++;        // star absorbs one more char
                si = matchIdx;
            } else {
                return false;
            }
        }
        while (pi < p.length() && p.charAt(pi) == '*') pi++;  // trailing stars
        return pi == p.length();
    }
}
```

This approach uses O(1) extra space but has the same O(m*n) worst-case time due to backtracking.

### What About Space Optimization for DP?

Since each row depends only on the previous row, we can reduce space from O(m*n) to O(n) using two rolling arrays.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class WildcardSpaceOpt {
    public static boolean isMatch(String s, String p) {
        int m = s.length(), n = p.length();
        boolean[] prev = new boolean[n + 1];
        prev[0] = true;
        for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
            if (p.charAt(j - 1) == '*') prev[j] = prev[j - 1];
        }

        for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++) {
            boolean[] curr = new boolean[n + 1];
            for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
                char pc = p.charAt(j - 1);
                if (pc == '*') {
                    curr[j] = prev[j] || curr[j - 1];
                } else if (pc == '?' || pc == s.charAt(i - 1)) {
                    curr[j] = prev[j - 1];
                }
            }
            prev = curr;
        }
        return prev[n];
    }
}
```

### What Are Common Interview Follow-Up Questions?

Interviewers commonly ask:

- Regex Matching: star modifies the preceding element instead of being standalone.
- Implement glob matching with `**` for directory recursion.
- Compare DP vs greedy: DP is cleaner, greedy uses O(1) space.
- Handle escaped characters: `\*` and `\?` should match literal star and question mark.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class WildcardFollowUps {
    // Regex: star tied to preceding element
    // Glob **: recursive directory matching
    // Escaped chars: treat \* as literal *
    // DP vs Greedy: same time, greedy is O(1) space
}
```
